\v 18 But¦103381 \add *they\add* didn't¦103381 \add ≈attack¦103382 those cities\add* because¦103385 \add *their\add* leaders¦103389 had¦103387 made a promise by¦103391 the¦103392 god¦103392 of Yisrael¦103384, Yahweh¦103391.
\p \add ≈All¦103393 the Israelis¦103384\add* grumbled¦103394 against their leaders for making that treaty,
\v 19 \add ≈but¦103402\add* their leaders¦103405 told \add *them\add*, “We¦103416 ourselves have¦103412 promised¦103412 them by¦103414 \add *our\add* God¦103415 Yahweh¦103414 \add ≈so\add* now¦103417 we¦103419 can't¦103418 touch¦103420 them.
\v 20 We're \add ≈forced now to let¦103426 them live¦103426, otherwise God's anger¦103432 would be¦103430 against us¦103431 for breaking our promise\add* to them.
\v 21 \add ≈So¦103441\add* we'll let¦103444 them live¦103444 \add but make them our slaves\add*.” \add ≈As a result of that,\add* \add @the Gibeonites\add* became woodcutters and¦103441 water-carriers¦103450 for¦103456 all the \add ≈Israelis\add*.
\p
